  for some $|\alpha_b|\leq 1,|\beta_c|\leq \log c$. Writing $\log n=\log b+\log(n/b)$, we can estimate $T_1$ with the type I bound. For $T_2$, we split $b,c$ into dyadic intervals $(N_1/2,N_1],(N_2/2,N_2]$ and observe that the sum is empty unless $N/2<N_1 N_2<4N'$. Next, we denote by $ \|\theta\|$ the distance of $\theta$ to the nearest integer and remove the cross-condition by 
  \begin{align}
    \label{eq:removcross}  \mathbf{1}_{N/2 < bc \leq N'}&=\int_0^1 \sum_{N/(2 c) \leq j \leq N'/c}e(\theta j) e(-\theta b) \,\mathrm{d} \theta\\
      & \nonumber =\int_0^1 \nu_{\theta,c} \min\{N'/c, \|\theta\|^{-1}\} e(-\theta b) \,\mathrm{d} \theta
  \end{align}
for some measurable function $|\nu_{\theta,c}|\leq 1$. Taking the maximum over $\theta$ and incorporating $\nu_{\theta,c}$ and $e(-\theta b)$ into the coefficients gives a valid type II sum after observing that either $N_1\leq 2\sqrt{N'}$ or $N_2\leq 2\sqrt{N'}$. 


\textbf{The case of $\Lambda_{E_3^*}$.}

If $\Lambda^{*}=\Lambda_{E_3^*}$, we follow the strategy in~\cite[Sections 10.2, 10.3]{matomaki-shao}. Recalling~\eqref{eq:c_E}, we can remove the weight $1/c_{E_3^*}(n)$ with Lemma~\ref{lem:partsum}. Afterwards, we are left with 
  \begin{align*}
