        =& \sum_{\substack{(q,\widetilde{r})=1\\ q>R/\widetilde{r}}} \frac{\mu(q)}{\varphi(q)}\left(\sum_{d\mid \widetilde{r}} \frac{\mu(d)^2}{\varphi(d)}G\left(\frac{\log dq}{\log R}\right)- \frac{\widetilde{r}}{\varphi(\widetilde{r})} G\left(\frac{\log \widetilde{r}q}{\log R}\right)\right)\,\,\,\sideset{}{^{\ast}}\sum_{a\pmod q}\,\sum_{n\leq N}n^{\widetilde{\beta}-1}e\left(n(\alpha-a/q)\right) \\
        \ll & \sum_{\substack{(q,\widetilde{r})=1\{\mathbb R}/\widetilde{r}<q\leq R^2}}\frac{R^{o(1)}}{q} \,\,\,\sideset{}{^{\ast}}\sum_{a\pmod q} \left| \sum_{n\leq N}n^{\widetilde{\beta}-1}e\left(n(\alpha-a/q\right)\right|.
    \end{align*}
    By partial summation (or~\cite[Proposition 5.3]{green-sarkozy}), the sum over $n$ is bounded by $\min\{N,\|\alpha-a/q\|^{-1}\}$. Observe that the appearing fractions $a/q$ are at least $R^{-4}$-spaced. The nearest fraction to $\alpha$ (with $q>R/\widetilde{r}$) we estimate trivially by $\min\{\cdot\}\le N$, contributing $\ll R^{o(1)}N/q\ll R^{o(1)}N\widetilde{r}/R$. The remaining $R^{-4}$-spaced fractions contribute $\ll R^{o(1)}\sum'\|\alpha-a/q\|^{-1}\ll R^{4+o(1)}$. Hence the previous expression is
    \begin{align*}
        {}\ll{} \frac{N R^{o(1)}\widetilde{r}}{R}+R^{4+o(1)}\ll  \frac{N R^{o(1)}\widetilde{r}}{R}.
    \end{align*}
    Thus,~\eqref{eq:roughapprE_3}  follows, and this was enough to complete the proof.

