Combining the terms and discarding the non-negative $n^{\widetilde{\beta}-1}$ term,~\eqref{eq_lem_finalex_1} follows. The remaining case~\eqref{eq_lem_finalex_2} is proved in the same way: we either immediately get $\approx_{\widetilde{\epsilon}}$ from Proposition~\ref{prop_reducetoroughexc} or have a factor of $(1-\widetilde{\beta})(\log N) $ that improves the approximation correspondingly.

				We partition the relevant $m$ according to whether $\sigma_1(m)\ge -1/2$ or $\sigma_1(m)<-1/2$, and bound the exceptional set within each class separately. As $0\leq \mathbf{1}_{\widetilde{\chi}(n)=-1} \leq 1$,
